course: dictionary
lesson: 11748
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Bail is one of them. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning is meant. «The judge set bail, so he could wait at home for his trial.» Here bail is money paid to a court, so the person can stay out of prison until the trial.
explain: «Mia grabbed a bucket and began to bail the rowing boat.» There is no money and no court here. Boats, buckets and water are the clues. Bail means to scoop water out of a boat.
explain: Bail can also mean to leave someone when they need you. «Ben said he would come, then bailed on us at the last minute.» The words «at the last minute» tell you Ben left suddenly, or did not turn up. Try your meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: Bail can be a noun (a payment to a court, or a cricket piece) or a verb (to scoop water out, or to leave suddenly).
fact: «Bail» in law means money paid to a court so a person can stay out of prison until the trial.
fact: «Bail» as a verb means to scoop water out of a boat with a bucket. In the UK it can also be spelled «bale».
fact: In cricket, the bails are the two small wooden pieces resting on top of the stumps.
fact: «Bail out» can mean to help someone out of trouble, often with money. «Bail on someone» is slang for leaving suddenly or not turning up.
exercise: «The judge set bail, so he could wait at home for his trial.» What does bail mean here? | *Money paid to a court so a person can stay out of prison | To leave suddenly | A small wooden piece on top of the stumps | To scoop water out of a boat
exercise: «Mia used a bucket to bail the water out of the rowing boat.» What does bail mean? | *To scoop water out of a boat | The small pieces on top of the stumps | To jump out of a plane | To help someone with money
exercise: Dad says, «Don't bail on me now!» What does bail on mean? | To hit the ball far | *To leave suddenly, or not turn up | To help someone with money | To scoop water out of a boat
test: «The batter was out when a bail flew off the stumps.» What is a bail in this sentence? | A payment to a court | A bucket for scooping water | A parachute | *One of the two small wooden pieces on top of the stumps
test: «Her parents helped her bail out her brother when he was in trouble.» What does bail out mean here? | *To help someone out of trouble, often with money | To jump out of a plane | To leave suddenly without a word | To scoop water out of a boat
exam: Which sentence uses bail to mean money paid to a court? | «They had to bail water all afternoon.» | *«He was released on bail until his trial.» | «I will bail on the party tonight.» | «The bails fell off the stumps.»
